{"name":"Ethereal Mist","hex":"#b0b8cc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ethereal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to the presupposition of an invisible air-like element permeating all of space, or to the higher regions beyond the earth or beyond the atmosphere.","Pertaining to the immaterial realm, as symbolically represented by, or (in earlier epochs) conflated with, such atmospheric and extra-atmospheric concepts."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of ethereal wave (“music genre”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ethereal"},{"term":"mist","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water or other liquid finely suspended in air.","A layer of fine droplets or particles."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form mist.","To spray fine droplets on, particularly of water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mist","blocks":[{"text":"Mist is a natural phenomenon caused by small droplets of water aerosols suspended in the cold air, usually by condensation. Physically, it is an example of a dispersion, most commonly seen where water vapor in warm, moist air meets sudden cooling, such as in exhaled air in the winter, or when hot sauna steam is suddenly released outside. Mist occurs naturally as part of weather, typically when humid air comes into contact with surfaces that are much cooler. It can also be created artificially with aerosol spray dispensers if the humidity and temperature conditions are right."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D0%B0_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BC_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mist","wikidata":"Q192196"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Chambord_au_lever_du_jour.jpg/500px-Chambord_au_lever_du_jour.